WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.104
My name is Felicia Williams, and I work at Facebook in augmented reality and virtual reality. Augmented reality is

00:00:15.104 --> 00:00:19.944
going to really change the way that we think about using computers, and it's

00:00:19.968 --> 00:00:23.968
a really fun place to be right now.

00:00:23.968 --> 00:00:28.888
Whether you're making an animation, an app, or a game, it's often necessary for your computer

00:00:28.888 --> 00:00:29.928
program to test

00:00:29.953 --> 00:00:33.313
a condition to see if it's true or false.

00:00:33.313 --> 00:00:35.893
To do this, we use a Boolean expression,

00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:39.833
an expression that can only evaluate to either true or false.

00:00:39.937 --> 00:00:43.977
This is similar to asking the computer a yes or no question.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:49.917
Let's look at three types of expressions that we can make with comparison operators.

00:00:49.922 --> 00:00:53.962
Two equal signs compares the values in each box and asks,

00:00:53.962 --> 00:00:56.942
are these two values equal to one another?

00:00:56.942 --> 00:00:59.882
The greater than sign compares two values and asks

00:00:59.907 --> 00:01:03.907
if the value on the left is greater than the value on the right.

00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.907
And finally, the less than sign compares the two values

00:01:06.907 --> 00:01:09.891
and asks if the value on the left is less than

00:01:09.891 --> 00:01:11.971
the value on the right.

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:16.931
For each comparison, we will get either a true or false answer.

00:01:16.931 --> 00:01:19.876
For example, when playing a game, you may get a bonus

00:01:19.876 --> 00:01:23.556
when you reach a specific score, say 100.

00:01:23.556 --> 00:01:25.596
To know when to give the bonus, the computer

00:01:25.596 --> 00:01:27.076
will use a Boolean expression that

00:01:27.076 --> 00:01:29.860
asks, is the value stored in the variable score

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:31.980
equal to 100?

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.620
If the expression evaluates to true, then you will get a bonus.

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:39.020
If it evaluates to false, nothing happens.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:39.845
We can use another

00:01:39.845 --> 00:01:44.785
comparison operator to make our game even more accurate. In this example, the

00:01:44.785 --> 00:01:49.805
bonus is only given if the player's score is exactly 100. But if the player

00:01:49.829 --> 00:01:53.229
passes 100, they should still get the bonus, right?

00:01:53.229 --> 00:01:56.269
To do this, we'll use the Boolean expression that asks,

00:01:56.269 --> 00:01:58.949
is the user's score greater than 99?

00:01:58.949 --> 00:01:59.814
If this is true,

00:01:59.814 --> 00:02:02.334
then the player gets a bonus.

00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:06.054
By using Boolean expressions to ask true or false questions,

00:02:06.054 --> 00:02:08.874
you can make your animations, apps, and games

00:02:08.874 --> 00:02:09.799
even more dynamic

00:02:09.799 --> 00:02:11.799
and interactive for your users.
